    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for the positive value of \\( t \\) in the equation \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we need to use the formula for the magnitude of a complex number. For a complex number \\( z = a + bi \\), the magnitude is given by \\( |z| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\). Here, \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) into the magnitude formula:\n\\[\n|{-4 + ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we set this equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}.\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n(-4)^2 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2.\n\\]\n\nSimplify both sides:\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13.\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( 4 \\cdot 13 \\):\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 13 = 52.\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52.\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( t^2 \\), subtract 16 from both sides:\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16.\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 36.\n\\]\n\nTake the square root of both sides to solve for \\( t \\). Since we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\):\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36}.\n\\]\n\\[\nt = 6.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the positive value of \\( t \\) that satisfies the equation is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
